---
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { page } from '../../config/pages';
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { facts } from '../../lib/facts';
import { EXTERNAL_REL } from '../../lib/links';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does Glideworth tell me which country I am tax resident in?',
    a: '<p>No. Residence is what you enter: you are resident where your timeline says. Glideworth applies no statutory residence test, no 183-day rule and no treaty tie-breaker, so decide your status first (with a professional if it is unclear) and then model it.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which countries have the most detailed treatment?',
    a: '<p>The United Kingdom for non-resident rules, and the UK-UAE treaty, which is implemented article by article. The United States, Canada, Australia, Spain, the UAE and the other countries have basic non-resident rules, and the other treaties are basic.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I do all of this in the web app?',
    a: '<p>Most of it, yes: the app has screens for the residency timeline, citizenship and past residence, a country on each item, tax by country, and a stay-or-move comparison. A few items are set differently or only through the API; they are listed on this page.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if my plan never moves?',
    a: '<p>Nothing here runs. A household that never moves and holds nothing abroad gets the same results as before the model existed; the engine checks this with a regression suite of 31 households.</p>',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  {...page('/planning/moving-country/')}
  heading="Planning a move between countries"
  eyebrow="Moving country"
  lede="Glideworth can model living in more than one country over a plan: where you live each year, which country taxes what, and what a move does to your tax and your final wealth."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Planning by country', href: '/planning/' },
    { label: 'Moving country', href: '/planning/moving-country/' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="container body">
    <Prose wide>
      <Callout type="warning" title="Estimates of the tax mechanics, not of your status">
        <p>
          Cross-border tax is an estimate of how tax works once you have decided where you are resident. Residence
          status is what you enter. Split years are prorated by months, and treaty relief assumes you have claimed it.
          Always check your own position with a qualified adviser.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h2 id="what-it-models">What it models</h2>
      <p>
        The engine keeps a residency timeline for each person, taxes what each country is entitled to tax, relieves double
        tax by treaty or credit, and shows the result by country and by reason. It covers {facts.taxCountryCount} countries with
        national tax models, and cross-border results are byte-identical to single-country results for any plan that
        never moves and holds nothing abroad.
      </p>

      <h2 id="timeline">The residency timeline and split years</h2>
      <p>
        Each person starts resident where the household is. You add moves to the plan: a month, a country and region, and
        whether it is one of you or both. A milestone can also carry a "relocate" effect, such as moving back home when you
        retire. Partners can live in different countries. You can also record where you lived before the plan starts, which
        the UK look-back tests need.
      </p>
      <p>
        A move part-way through a tax year splits it. Each country taxes its share of the year by months, and pay is taxed by
        the country you live in while you earn it: pay stops in the old country at the move. The real UK split-year
        treatment, which can keep a full personal allowance, is approximated by this proration.
      </p>

      <h2 id="source-taxation">Tax on income from a country you have left</h2>
      <p>
        The country you live in taxes your worldwide income. A country where something is located may also tax it as a
        non-resident. Each account, property, debt and income has a country, defaulting to your household's, and it does not
        change when you move: a UK pension stays a UK pension.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>United Kingdom (detailed).</strong> Non-residents keep the personal allowance only if entitled (British or EEA
          citizens, or by treaty). Non-resident capital gains tax applies to UK property from the April 2015 or April 2019
          value. The model also covers ISAs and pension rules.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>United States (basic).</strong> US citizens and green-card holders pay US tax on worldwide income wherever they
          live, after the foreign earned income exclusion (prorated by months abroad) and a foreign tax credit, in one
          basket without carry-forward. A US person abroad pays federal tax only, not state tax.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Canada, Australia, Spain, the UAE and the others (basic).</strong> Country-specific rules for pay, rent,
          property gains and some withholding, not every condition. Where a rule is missing, the resident rates are used.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="treaties">Treaty relief</h2>
      <p>
        The residence country credits tax paid to the source country, limited to its own tax on the same income, so nothing is
        taxed twice. Treaties decide who keeps the right to tax each kind of income.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>UK-UAE is the detailed one</strong>, implemented from the 2016 convention article by article (Articles 4, 6, 10,
          11, 13, 14, 17, 18, 20 and 21): pay is taxed where the work is done; private pensions, interest and dividends only
          in the country of residence; the State Pension under the pensions article (residence only); government pensions
          in the paying state; rent and property gains where the property is; other gains and other income in the country
          of residence. The convention was signed on 12 April 2016 and has effect from 2017.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Basic treaties</strong> cover UK-US, UK-Australia, UK-Canada, UK-Spain, UK-France and US-Canada. They record
          the main allocation rules, not each article, and some rows are marked "not verified" and use the credit method.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Any other pair</strong> falls back to both countries taxing under domestic law with a credit in the country
          of residence, and Glideworth shows a warning.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Treaty relief is assumed claimed, for example with a form such as HMRC's DT-Individual; until it is, the source country
        may deduct tax.
      </p>

      <h2 id="leaving-and-arriving">Leaving and arriving</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Departure tax.</strong> Leaving Canada triggers a deemed sale of taxable investment accounts, and leaving
          Australia triggers capital gains tax event I1. Both are taxed on the value at the start of the year you leave, and you
          can model the deferral elections. Registered plans, such as an RRSP or a TFSA, are excluded.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Arrival rebasing.</strong> Arriving in Canada or Australia resets the cost of your investments to market value
          without tax.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wrappers.</strong> You cannot pay into a UK ISA while you live abroad, from the tax year after you leave. UK
          pension contributions get relief on up to £3,600 gross for five tax years after leaving, then stop. Canadian TFSA and
          RRSP contributions stop. A new country may not recognise a foreign tax-free account, so by default Glideworth taxes the
          growth when you take money out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exchange rates.</strong> Tax in each country is worked out in its currency with your rate table. Add a rate for
          every currency involved; a missing rate is a warning and treated as 1.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="uk-rules">UK temporary non-residence, State Pension and inheritance tax</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Temporary non-residence.</strong> If you were UK resident in four of the seven tax years before leaving and are
          away for five years or less, gains you realise abroad are taxed in the year you return. This covers capital gains
          only, not pension lump sums.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Frozen State Pension.</strong> The UK State Pension is uprated each year in the EEA, Switzerland and some other
          countries, and frozen at the rate first paid where it is not (the UAE, Canada, Australia and others). It goes back to
          the current rate if you return. An audit finding tells you when you are short of qualifying years and what voluntary
          Class 3 contributions would cost: £18.40 a week in 2026-27, needing ten years of UK residence or ten qualifying years. From
          2026-27 you cannot pay Class 2 for time abroad.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Inheritance tax.</strong> From 6 April 2025, someone UK resident in at least 10 of the previous 20 tax years is a
          long-term resident, and their worldwide estate is in scope. After you leave, it stays in scope for 3 to 10 tax years,
          depending on how long you lived in the UK; after that, only UK assets.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="you-enter">What you enter yourself</h2>
      <p>
        Glideworth does not decide your status. You enter:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Residence</strong> over time, since there is no statutory residence test, 183-day rule or tie-breaker.</li>
        <li><strong>Citizenships</strong> and any US green card, where you lived before the plan, and state-pension records with qualifying years and voluntary years you plan to buy.</li>
        <li><strong>The country</strong> of each account, property, debt and income.</li>
        <li><strong>Exchange rates</strong> for each currency involved.</li>
        <li><strong>Voluntary contributions</strong> as an expense yourself; they are not added automatically.</li>
        <li>A gratuity or bonus paid after you move is taxed where you then live. The end-of-service gratuity is UAE-only.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="stay-or-move">Stay or move?</h2>
      <p>
        The stay-or-move comparison runs two deterministic projections, one where you stay and one where you move, and shows the
        plan changes, the lifetime tax by country and reason, and where the difference in your final wealth comes from. It is
        deterministic, not a chance of success: branch the plan to run Monte Carlo on each.
      </p>

      <h2 id="where">Where each part is today</h2>
      <p>
        The engine models all of the above. The web app has screens for most of it, and the API and MCP server expose the rest.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>What</th>
            <th>In the web app</th>
            <th>Through the API and MCP</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Residency timeline and moves</td>
            <td>Plan editor, "Where you live", and a "Relocate" effect on milestones</td>
            <td>Residency routes under a plan; <code>get_residency_timeline</code>, <code>propose_move</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cross-border switches (rules on or off, departure tax, UK temporary non-residence, State Pension freeze)</td>
            <td>Plan editor, "Where you live"</td>
            <td>Plan tax settings</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Citizenship, past residence, state-pension records</td>
            <td>Current finances, "About you", "Citizenship, past residence and state pension"</td>
            <td>A per-person cross-border record; <code>set_person_cross_border</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Country of an account, property, debt or income</td>
            <td>A "Country" field on each. Planned real assets and plan-level account overrides have no country field in the plan editor: set it on the item in Current finances</td>
            <td>Cross-border items routes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tax by country and reason; "How this is taxed, and why"</td>
            <td>Taxes page and the Results year drill-down. The bracket and marginal-rate analytics on the Taxes page describe the household country's calculation only</td>
            <td>Tax breakdown by country; <code>tax_by_country</code>, <code>explain_item_taxation</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Stay or move, and the relocation what-if</td>
            <td>Insights, "Stay or move?"</td>
            <td>A stay-or-move insight; <code>compare_stay_or_move</code>; a relocation template</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Rule tables (non-resident rules, treaties)</td>
            <td>Appear in "How this is taxed, and why", with the treaty article and its source, and as warnings</td>
            <td>Cross-border rule tables; <code>cross_border_rules</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="tables">Tables and tax years</h2>
      <p>
        UK non-resident rules are for 2026-27 (gov.uk). UK inheritance tax long-term residence is from 6 April 2025. Voluntary
        National Insurance is for 2026-27 and the US foreign earned income exclusion for 2025 and 2026. Each country's own tables
        are on its <a href="/planning/">country page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="questions">Questions</h2>
      <FAQ items={faq} />

      <h2 id="sources">Official sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/united-arab-emirates-tax-treaties/2016-uk-uae-double-taxation-convention" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>UK-UAE Double Taxation Convention (2016)</a> and <a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/double-taxation-relief/dt19752" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>HMRC DT19752</a></li>
        <li>Other UK treaties: <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/usa-tax-treaties" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>USA</a>, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/australia-tax-treaties" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Australia</a>, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/canada-tax-treaties" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Canada</a>, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/spain-tax-treaties" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Spain</a> and <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/france-tax-treaties" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>France</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/guidance/inheritance-tax-if-youre-a-long-term-uk-resident" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Inheritance tax if you are a long-term UK resident</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/voluntary-national-insurance-contributions/rates" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Voluntary National Insurance rates</a> and <a href="https://www.gov.uk/voluntary-national-insurance-contributions/if-you-live-or-work-abroad" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>living or working abroad</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/state-pensions-annual-increases-if-you-live-abroad/countries-where-we-pay-an-annual-increase-in-the-state-pension" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Countries where the State Pension is increased</a>, which may be out of date</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/international-non-residents/individuals-leaving-entering-canada-non-residents/dispositions-property.html" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Canada: dispositions of property for emigrants</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.ato.gov.au/individuals-and-families/investments-and-assets/capital-gains-tax/foreign-residents-and-capital-gains-tax/how-changing-residency-affects-cgt" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>Australia: how changing residency affects CGT</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/figuring-the-foreign-earned-income-exclusion" rel={EXTERNAL_REL}>IRS: foreign earned income exclusion</a></li>
      </ul>

      <Callout type="note" title="Estimates, not advice">
        <p>
          Glideworth is planning software. Its tax figures help you compare choices under your own assumptions; they are not a
          tax return and not tax advice.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </Prose>
    <RelatedLinks />
  </div>
  <CTABand />
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .body {
    padding-bottom: var(--section-y);
  }
</style>
